// The fasteners (G2.2, ADR-0033): what holds parts together where they touch. Each joint's law
// (stiffness, slip, strength, failure) is worked out from its entry here and the two parts'
// woods (creeer_building::law): nothing in a joint is written down beyond these.
//
// HOW TO ADD ONE (data work; `creeer_tools validate` checks every entry):
// - `name`: unique id; `material`: an engineering material's id (data/materials/engineering.ron).
// - `kind`, one of:
//   - Nail(diameter, length, head): a smooth round nail, driven without a bored hole (m). Its
//     wire's tensile strength in `strength` (Pa): drawn wire is far stronger than its stock
//     (EN 10230 nails: at least 600 MPa).
//   - Peg(diameter, length): a wooden dowel in a bored hole (m); `strength` none (its wood's
//     bending strength is the material's).
//   - Lashing(width, thickness, wraps, tack, pretension, friction): a strip or cord (m), turns round
//     both parts, how many of them one hand makes while the other holds the part in place (a
//     tack, G2.10: they hold it until the rest are drawn with both hands; 0: it cannot be
//     tacked), the share of its strength it is drawn to by hand, and the friction between
//     the parts it clamps.
//   Bolts and welds come as kinds of their own later (EN 1993-1-8): a law each in law.rs, and
//   entries here.
// - `peak_slip`, `failure_slip`: how far the joint slips at its peak force and when it fails
//   (m). Estimates where marked; test data (load–slip curves) refine them.
// - `seconds`: how long fastening one takes by hand, its tool at hand: short and seen (the user,
//   2026-10-30: a nail about 2 s, a lashing about 8 s, the part held still meanwhile), not the
//   real half minute of a lashing: building is not to be waited on.
[
    // A 3.1 × 75 mm round wire nail (EN 10230), its head 7 mm. Slips: smooth nails in softwood
    // reach their peak near 8 mm and fail near 15 mm in lateral tests (Foschi 1974's curves;
    // estimates to be refined against EN 1380 test data).
    (name: "nail_75", kind: Nail(diameter: 0.0031, length: 0.075, head: 0.007), material: "steel_s235", strength: Some(600.0e6), peak_slip: 0.008, failure_slip: 0.015, seconds: 2.0),
    // A 4.0 × 100 mm round wire nail, head 9 mm (same sources).
    (name: "nail_100", kind: Nail(diameter: 0.004, length: 0.100, head: 0.009), material: "steel_s235", strength: Some(600.0e6), peak_slip: 0.010, failure_slip: 0.020, seconds: 2.0),
    // A 25 mm birch peg, 200 mm long: a trenail, as timber framers drive (Sandberg, Bulleit &
    // Reid 2000 tested 25.4 mm oak pegs: ductile, failing past 10–20 mm of slip; estimates).
    (name: "peg_25", kind: Peg(diameter: 0.025, length: 0.200), material: "birch_green", strength: None, peak_slip: 0.010, failure_slip: 0.020, seconds: 10.0),
    // A lashing of fresh spruce bast, a 12 × 2 mm strip, four turns drawn to a quarter of its
    // strength by hand (estimate), wood on wood μ 0.4 (Wood Handbook: 0.3–0.5). Lashed cross
    // joints slip far before they fail (BioResources 2024, lashed bamboo; estimates). Two turns
    // tack it, one hand holding the part (G2.10: as a lone builder starts a square lashing;
    // made at the same pace, no timing of one-handed turns being found).
    (name: "bast_lashing", kind: Lashing(width: 0.012, thickness: 0.002, wraps: 4, tack: 2, pretension: 0.25, friction: 0.4), material: "spruce_bast", strength: None, peak_slip: 0.010, failure_slip: 0.030, seconds: 8.0),
]
